## Project State Summary

**Problem:** go-unix-utils is a specification-driven experiment that regenerates GNU Unix utilities in Go, verified via differential testing against Homebrew reference binaries. The goal is to stress-test the mage-claude-orchestrator code generation pipeline.

**Architecture:** Four-stage pipeline (extract → specify → synthesize → verify). Shared libraries in pkg/ (testutils, sys, format), per-utility packages in cmd/, specifications in docs/. Human writes specs; orchestrator generates code; differential testing validates parity.

**Implementation status:** 17 commands implemented across generations 1–17 (ts, head, seq, echo, tac, nl, fold, expand, unexpand, cut, paste, uniq, comm, md5sum, sha1sum, sha256sum, sha512sum) plus foundation packages. All releases through rel05.4 are marked "implemented" in code_status. Code lives at generation-merged tags, not on the working branch.

**Current release scope:** rel99.2 — deferred release containing one use case: rel99.2-uc001-ts. Status is "spec_complete" (PRD, use case, and test suite exist; code generation has not run on the current branch).

## Priority Analysis

rel99.2 is the only release in scope. It has a single use case (ts). ts was previously generated in early runs but its code is only at generation-merged tags, not on the working branch. The spec is complete and ready for code generation.

ts is a moreutils utility (timestamp prepender). It depends on pkg/sys (SIGPIPE handler) which already exists. No other pkg/ dependencies needed. Since implementation and tests must be split (P2), and I'm limited to 1 task, I propose the implementation task. The test task follows in a future session.

ts is a moreutils tool, so the reference binary (`ts` from Homebrew moreutils) may not be available in stitch containers — the test task (future) will need the graceful-skip pattern.

```yaml
- index: 0
  title: "cmd/ts implementation -- rel99.2-uc001-ts"
  dependency: -1
  description: |
    deliverable_type: code

    required_reading:
      - docs/specs/product-requirements/prd005-ts.yaml (all requirements for the ts utility)
      - docs/specs/use-cases/rel99.2-uc001-ts.yaml (end-to-end execution path)
      - docs/specs/test-suites/test-rel-99.2.yaml (test cases that validate acceptance)
      - docs/ARCHITECTURE.yaml (shared_protocols section -- differential testing pattern, SIGPIPE handling)
      - pkg/sys/sigpipe.go (InstallSIGPIPEHandler usage pattern)
      - cmd/tac/main.go (reference for stdin-reading utility structure and SIGPIPE setup)
      - cmd/nl/main.go (reference for line-by-line stdin transform with formatting)

    files:
      - path: cmd/ts/main.go
        action: create
        note: ts utility entry point -- stdin line-by-line timestamp prepender

    requirements:
      - id: R1
        text: |
          Implement the ts command that reads lines from stdin and prepends a
          timestamp to each line before writing to stdout. When no format
          argument is given, use the default strftime format from the PRD.
          Implement all flags specified in prd005-ts (typically -s for elapsed
          time since start, -i for incremental time since previous line, and
          -m for monotonic clock source). Follow the PRD for exact flag names,
          defaults, and semantics.
      - id: R2
        text: |
          Parse the optional strftime format string from the first positional
          argument. Apply Go time.Format equivalents for all strftime
          directives specified in the PRD. When -s or -i is active, the format
          string applies to the elapsed duration representation as defined in
          the PRD.
      - id: R3
        text: |
          Handle stream lifecycle correctly: read stdin until EOF, flush each
          output line immediately (no buffering beyond the line boundary), and
          exit 0 on success. Call pkg/sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler() at the start
          of main so piped output to a consumer that closes early (e.g.,
          head) exits cleanly.
      - id: R4
        text: |
          Implement GNU-compatible error handling and exit codes as specified
          in the PRD. Write usage/help to stderr on invalid arguments and exit
          with the exit code the PRD defines for argument errors.

    design_decisions:
      - id: D1
        text: |
          Use a single main.go file in cmd/ts/. The ts utility is small enough
          that all logic fits in one file. Follow the same structure as other
          cmd/ packages: main() calls sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler(), parses
          flags, then enters the read-transform-write loop.
      - id: D2
        text: |
          Use Go's time package for wall-clock timestamps and
          time.Now().Sub(start) for elapsed/incremental modes. For monotonic
          clock (-m flag), rely on Go's built-in monotonic clock reading
          embedded in time.Time values (no CGO or syscall needed).
      - id: D3
        text: |
          Use bufio.Scanner for line-by-line stdin reading and
          bufio.NewWriter(os.Stdout) for output, flushing after each line.
          This matches the pattern used by other line-oriented utilities in
          cmd/ (nl, tac).
      - id: D4
        text: |
          Map strftime directives to Go time.Format layout strings. Go does
          not have a strftime function; build a conversion table for the
          directives the PRD requires. Only implement directives listed in
          the PRD -- do not attempt full strftime coverage.

    acceptance_criteria:
      - id: AC1
        text: cmd/ts/main.go compiles with no errors via "go build ./cmd/ts/"
      - id: AC2
        text: "echo 'hello' | go run ./cmd/ts/ prints a timestamp-prefixed line to stdout"
      - id: AC3
        text: The -s flag produces elapsed timestamps relative to program start
      - id: AC4
        text: The -i flag produces incremental timestamps relative to the previous line
      - id: AC5
        text: A custom strftime format string as the first positional argument changes the output format
      - id: AC6
        text: The binary exits 0 on normal EOF and handles SIGPIPE without error output
      - id: AC7
        text: golangci-lint reports no warnings on cmd/ts/
```